  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="d6ecaf8b-a27d-47f2-950b-bf6cbc2c53a0" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Use the following scenario to answer questions 1 – 5.</para>
            <para>
              <quote>Based on past musical productions, a theater predicts selling \(400-8p\) tickets when each ticket is sold at \(p\) dollars.</quote>
           </para>
            <para>Answer questions a–g to complete the middle column in the table to find out how many tickets the theater expects to sell and what revenues it expects to receive at the given ticket prices.</para>
            <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
              <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Ticket price (dollars)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Number of tickets sold</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>Revenue (dollars)</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(5\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(360\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(1800\)</para>
                  </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(10\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>a. _____</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(15\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>b. _____</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(20\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>c. _____</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(30\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>d. _____</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(45\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>e. _____</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(50\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>f. _____</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(p\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>g. _____</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
            <para>When the price is \($10\) per ticket, what is the number of tickets sold?</para>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! There are 320 tickets sold when the price is $10 per ticket.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.7.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 320. There are 320 tickets sold when the price is $10 per ticket.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_217116ac-3762-41b7-adab-fb18faba6211" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>When the price is \($15\) per ticket, what is the number of tickets sold?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! There are 280 tickets sold when the price is $15 per ticket.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.7.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 280. There are 280 tickets sold when the price is $15 per ticket.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
