          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_92dbe1fe-ff71-442a-857a-d103d55e2fe0" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> When the price is \(p\) per ticket, what is the revenue in dollars?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(p(8p-400)\)</para>
            <para>\(400-8p\)</para>
            <para>\(p(400-8p)\)</para>
            <para>\(392p\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! \(p(400-8p)\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.7.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \(p(400-8p)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_07c98345-4d1e-48fb-a140-5262be95feb6" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>For which ticket price, listed in the table, will the theater earn no revenue?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The revenue is zero when the ticket price is $50.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.7.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 50. The revenue is zero when the ticket price is $50.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_0fb440fa-4233-44d8-a119-a040725ecaa2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>Calculate the lowest price of a ticket that the theater should sell the tickets for if it must earn at least \($3,200\) in revenue to break even (to not lose money) on the musical production. (The calculated value may not be listed in the table.)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The lowest price the theater should charge to at least make $3,200 and break even is $10 per ticket.</para>
            <para> Try again. Revisit activity 7.7.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 10. The lowest price the theater should charge to at least make $3,200 and break even is $10 per ticket.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="fa2f0344-3f29-441e-b9f9-d90c4cda33a2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>Calculate the highest price of a ticket that the theater should sell the tickets for if it must earn at least \($3,200\) in revenue to break even (to not lose money) on the musical production. (The calculated value may not be listed in the table.)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The highest price the theater should charge to make at least $3,200 and break even is $40 per ticket. Although not shown in the table, we can calculate that when \(p\) is $40, the expected revenue is \(40(400-8(40))\) or \(40 \cdot 80\), which is $3,200.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.7.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 40. The highest price the theater should charge to at least make $3,200 and break even is $40 per ticket. Although not shown in the table, we can calculate that when \(p\) is $40, the expected revenue is \(40(400-8(40)\) or \(40 \cdot 80\), which is $3,200.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_7617ff65-c7d1-4155-a872-5ae056ca9c63" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>A company sells running shoes. If the price of a pair of shoes in dollars is \(p\), the company estimates that it will sell \(50,000-400p\) pairs of shoes.
          
          Which expression represents the revenue in dollars from selling running shoes if a pair of shoes is priced at \(p\) dollars?
       </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\((50,000-400p) \cdot p\)</para>
            <para>\((50,000p-400) \cdot p\)</para>
            <para>\((50,000p-400p)\)</para>
            <para>\((49,600p) \cdot p\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \((50,000-400p) \cdot p\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.7.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \((50,000-400p) \cdot p\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_3cf96af7-0f03-45ae-8e35-07252044c9c1" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Use the following scenario to answer questions 7 – 10.</para>
            <para>
              <quote>The function \(f\) represents the revenue in dollars the school can expect to receive if it sells \(220-12x\) coffee mugs for \(x\) dollars each.</quote>
           </para>
            <para>Here is the graph of \(f\).</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Parabola on grid. Horizontal axis, price in dollars, from 0 to 20. Vertical axis, revenue in dollars, from 0 to 1000. Parabola opens down. Vertex near 9 comma 1000. \(\(x\)-intercepts near 0 and 18 point 2.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_09_10_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>Select <emphasis>three</emphasis> statements that describe this situation.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The school expects to sell 160 mugs if the price is $5 each. The school will earn about $1,000 if it sells the mugs for $10 each. The revenue will be more than $700 if the price is between $4 and $14.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.7.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answers are: The school expects to sell 160 mugs if the price is $5 each. The school will earn about $1,000 if it sells the mugs for $10 each. The revenue will be more than $700 if the price is between $4 and $14.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_5d533ed9-4520-4ef5-a252-6d5b25ea619c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>To the nearest hundred dollars, what is the maximum revenue for the mugs?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 1000</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.7.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 1,000.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="f919514b-7f1b-4f68-9eb8-bc27744cab64" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which is the approximate domain?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(0 \leq x \leq 9\)</para>
            <para>\(0 \leq x \leq 18\)</para>
            <para>\(0 \leq x \leq 1000\)</para>
            <para>\(x \geq 0\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(0 \leq x \leq 18\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.7.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(0 \leq x \leq 18\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="cf6b8ca6-cc68-40a3-93df-6aeef751887d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which is the approximate range?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(0 \leq y \lt \infty\)</para>
            <para>\(0 \leq y \leq 1000\)</para>
            <para>\(y \leq 1000\)</para>
            <para>\(-\infty \lt y \leq 1000\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(0 \leq y \leq 1000\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.7.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(0 \leq y \leq 1000\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
